Student Response:
1)The class definition is compact, and all the functions of the class are listed together.

2)A function(A) defined before another function(B) can also call B because they have been declared before. Eg:

Case 1: Gives error

void A(){
B();
}

void B()
{
cout << "Hello World!;
}

int main()
{ 
A();
return 0;
}

Case 2: Works! (Prints Hello World)

void A();
void B();

void A(){
B();
}

void B(){
cout << " Hello World";
}

int main()
{
A();
return 0;
}

