Question:
Two      advantages of separating declaration from definition are: .....

Rubric:
Score 2 (Correct): Clearly and specifically addresses the key requirement by covering overriding functions. enhances modularity code. also separates abstraction implementation, easier reader understand. allows easy changing, and code will more clear with accurate details.
Score 1 (Partial): Covers some key elements (at least 2 of: overriding functions. enhances modularity code. also separates abstraction implementation, easier reader understand. allows easy changing, and code will more clear) but lacks clarity or misses essential detail.
Score 0 (Incorrect): Does not address the key requirement or fails to mention overriding functions. enhances modularity code. also separates abstraction implementation, easier reader understand. allows easy changing, and code will more clear.

Student Response:
Some      advantages of separating declaration from definition are:

1) If declarations have been written together in the program, then when a user sees the code file, he'll have an easy time figuring out what all methods are available in the program. This leads to a "good-looking" program (maybe called clean/structured)

2) Suppose I have two methods f() and g() wherein f wants to use g and g  wants to use f. then we have to declare them both together first and then we can use them within each other, otherwise we cannot. 

e.g. 
int f(int p);
int g(int p);

/*if we don't do the above declaration the following code will throw error. */

int f(int p){
if(p>0){ /* do something without g*/}
else{ return g(p); }
}
int g(int p){
if(p>0){return f(p);}
else{/* do something without f */}
}

